WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.459
Detective Fix had been watching Mr. Fogg and Aouda since their arrival at the quay.

2
00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:12.578
He approached Mr. Fogg innocently and asked, "Were you not, like me sir, a passenger on the Rangoon

3
00:00:12.579 --> 00:00:17.040
?" "I was," replied Mr. Fogg. "But we have not been

4
00:00:17.041 --> 00:00:21.987
introduced." "Pardon me," said Fix. "I thought I would find your servant

5
00:00:21.988 --> 00:00:25.655
here." "Do you know where he is, sir?" asked Aouda

6
00:00:25.656 --> 00:00:30.859
anxiously. "What?" responded Fix, pretending to be surprised. "He is not with

7
00:00:30.860 --> 00:00:35.206
you?" "No," said Aouda. "We have not seen him since yesterday.

8
00:00:35.207 --> 00:00:38.197
Could he have boarded the Carnatic without us?"

9
00:00:38.198 --> 00:00:43.047
"Did you intend on sailing on the Carnatic as well?" asked Fix.

10
00:00:43.048 --> 00:00:46.662
"I am very disappointed, but I have missed it also.

11
00:00:46.663 --> 00:00:49.969
Apparently there won't be another steamer for a week."

12
00:00:49.970 --> 00:00:56.027
"But there are other vessels in the harbor besides the Carnatic ," replied Mr. Fogg with

13
00:00:56.028 --> 00:01:01.772
confidence. He held out his arm to Aouda, and they walked off. Fix grew

14
00:01:01.773 --> 00:01:05.316
alarmed. He had not expected Fogg to be so

15
00:01:05.317 --> 00:01:12.697
determined. For three hours Fix watched Mr. Fogg and Aouda wander the docks looking for a ship. He began to hope

16
00:01:12.698 --> 00:01:18.190
again. Then a sailor approached Mr. Fogg. "Is Your Honor looking for a boat?" he

17
00:01:18.191 --> 00:01:21.662
asked. "Do you have a boat ready to sail?" asked Mr.

18
00:01:21.663 --> 00:01:25.618
Fogg. "Yes, your honor," replied the sailor. "The best in the

19
00:01:25.619 --> 00:01:29.718
harbor." "Will you agree to take me to Yokohama?" asked Mr.

20
00:01:29.719 --> 00:01:34.269
Fogg. "Is Your Honor joking?" said the sailor. "It is

21
00:01:34.270 --> 00:01:40.576
impossible." "I have missed the Carnatic , and I must get to Yokohama by November 14," said

22
00:01:40.577 --> 00:01:47.168
Fogg. "I offer you one-hundred pounds per day and an additional two-hundred pounds if I reach Yokohama in time."

23
00:01:47.169 --> 00:01:50.054
"Are you in earnest?" asked the sailor.

24
00:01:50.055 --> 00:01:53.157
"Very much so," said Mr. Fogg.

25
00:01:53.158 --> 00:01:57.213
The sailor walked away a little distance and gazed out to sea.

26
00:01:57.214 --> 00:02:02.874
He was struggling between the temptation of such a large sum and the danger of traveling so far.

27
00:02:02.875 --> 00:02:09.960
Fix watched in suspense. Mr. Fogg turned to Aouda and asked her, "You would not be afraid, would you, madam?"

28
00:02:09.961 --> 00:02:12.611
"Not with you, Mr. Fogg," she answered.

29
00:02:12.612 --> 00:02:16.722
"Well, sailor?" said Mr. Fogg.

30
00:02:16.723 --> 00:02:22.609
"Your Honor, I could not risk myself, my men, or my little boat on such a long voyage at this time of year.

31
00:02:22.610 --> 00:02:27.006
Besides, we would not reach Yokohama in time," answered the sailor.

32
00:02:27.007 --> 00:02:29.130
Fix breathed more freely.

33
00:02:29.131 --> 00:02:33.939
"But," added the sailor, "it might be arranged another way."

34
00:02:33.940 --> 00:02:35.944
Fix held his breath.

35
00:02:35.945 --> 00:02:38.379
"How?" asked Mr. Fogg.

36
00:02:38.380 --> 00:02:41.527
"By going to Shanghai," said the sailor. "Sir,

37
00:02:41.528 --> 00:02:47.639
I must take the American steamer at Yokohama, not at Shanghai," said Mr. Fogg. "It

38
00:02:47.640 --> 00:02:51.478
isn't necessary to go as far as Yokohama," said the sailor. "The

39
00:02:51.479 --> 00:02:56.800
San Francisco steamer does not start from Yokohama. It begins at Shanghai." "You

40
00:02:56.801 --> 00:02:59.852
are sure of that?" asked Mr. Fogg. "Perfectly!"

41
00:02:59.853 --> 00:03:02.169
replied the sailor. "And

42
00:03:02.170 --> 00:03:05.643
when does the boat leave Shanghai?" asked Fogg. "On

43
00:03:05.644 --> 00:03:11.065
November 11, at seven in the evening," said the sailor. "We have four days before us." "And

44
00:03:11.066 --> 00:03:14.098
when can we leave?" asked Mr. Fogg. "In

45
00:03:14.099 --> 00:03:16.784
an hour," the sailor replied. "It

46
00:03:16.785 --> 00:03:20.799
is a deal. Are you the master of the boat?" asked Fogg. "Yes,"

47
00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:26.602
replied the sailor. "My name is John Bunsby, captain of the Tankadere ."

48
00:03:26.603 --> 00:03:31.808
The two men sealed their deal and Mr. Fogg gave John Bunsby a sum of money in advance.

49
00:03:31.809 --> 00:03:36.871
"But poor Passepartout!" urged Aouda. "We can't leave him behind."

50
00:03:36.872 --> 00:03:40.620
"I will do all I can to find him," replied Mr. Fogg.

51
00:03:40.621 --> 00:03:47.310
Before heading out in search of Passepartout, Mr. Fogg offered to let Mr. Fix join them as a passenger on the Tankadere .

52
00:03:47.311 --> 00:03:56.226
Mr. Fix could do nothing but accept. In a nervous state, Fix boarded the Tankadere while Mr. Fogg and Aouda went to the local police station.

53
00:03:56.227 --> 00:03:59.883
Phileas Fogg left Passepartout's description with the police.

54
00:03:59.884 --> 00:04:04.245
He also left a sum of money to be spent in search of him.

55
00:04:04.246 --> 00:04:06.680
By now it was three o'clock.

56
00:04:06.681 --> 00:04:15.498
They had stopped at the hotel, picked up their luggage, and arrived at the Tankadere just as the last of the boat's supplies were being stored.

57
00:04:15.499 --> 00:04:19.051
The Tankadere was a little boat with two tall masts.

58
00:04:19.052 --> 00:04:24.268
Its deck was as white as ivory, and all its brass fittings were polished to a shine.

59
00:04:24.269 --> 00:04:30.288
The boat was as graceful as a racing yacht, and it was obvious the captain took pride in it.

60
00:04:30.289 --> 00:04:33.754
Mr. Fogg and Aouda found Fix already on board.

61
00:04:33.755 --> 00:04:37.821
Below deck was a square cabin with cots set in the walls.

62
00:04:37.822 --> 00:04:44.281
In the center was a table with a swinging lamp above. The accommodations were small but neat. "I

63
00:04:44.282 --> 00:04:50.537
am sorry to have nothing better to offer you," said Mr. Fogg to Fix, who bowed without responding. The

64
00:04:50.538 --> 00:04:55.157
detective felt humiliated for relying on the kindness of Mr. Fogg. "He

65
00:04:55.158 --> 00:05:00.166
may be a rascal," thought Fix, "but he is a polite one!" The

66
00:05:00.167 --> 00:05:03.071
sails were raised at ten minutes past three. Mr.

67
00:05:03.072 --> 00:05:10.121
Fogg and Aouda, who were seated on the deck, cast a last glance at the quay in the hopes of seeing Passepartout. The

68
00:05:10.122 --> 00:05:16.497
Frenchman, however, did not appear. Passepartout's

69
00:05:16.498 --> 00:05:22.150
Guidebook You can imagine my disappointment when I found out that I had missed seeing Shanghai with Mr. Fogg and

70
00:05:22.151 --> 00:05:29.863
Aouda. Although considering the way Mr. Fogg travels, I guess I wouldn't have seen much of it anyway. Oh, how

71
00:05:29.864 --> 00:05:33.472
unfortunate! For many years Shanghai was a small Chinese

72
00:05:33.473 --> 00:05:37.831
port. Before the nineteenth century, Asian countries had little to do with the

73
00:05:37.832 --> 00:05:40.253
West. They preferred to trade with each

74
00:05:40.254 --> 00:05:45.932
other. But during the first part of this century, Asian countries felt more pressure to begin trading with the West.

75
00:05:45.933 --> 00:05:52.103
When countries like China and Japan refused, European and American armies sometimes attacked them!

76
00:05:52.104 --> 00:05:55.730
As if attacking a country would make it more willing to do business with you!

77
00:05:55.731 --> 00:06:02.017
In 1839 Britain and China went to war over the opium trade, and three years later, Britain won.

78
00:06:02.018 --> 00:06:06.497
As a result, the port of Shanghai was opened to international trade.

79
00:06:06.498 --> 00:06:14.584
Many foreigners settled there, mainly British, French, and American citizens.

